
~(exit-found)

#west-of-house
(name *)        West of House
(room *)
(look *)        You are in an open field west of a big white house, with a boarded front door.
(notice #front-door)
(* is sacred)
(from * go #north to #north-of-house)
(from * go #south to #south-of-house)
(from * go #west to #forest1)
%% TODO: merge with front-door
(narrate failing to leave * #east)
    (narrate door is locked)
(unlikely [go to *])
(very unlikely [go to *])

#mailbox
(* is #in #west-of-house)
(* is #in #mailbox)
(name *)        small mailbox
(container *)
(openable *)
(dict *)       mail-box mail box
(* capacity is 10)
(appearance *)  There is a small mailbox here. (list contents of *)

#leaflet
(* is #in #mailbox)
(name *)        leaflet
(item *)
(flammable *)
(readable *)
(* size is 2)
(dict *)        pamphlet booklet
(descr *)
    (div @headline) { WELCOME TO DUNGEON }
    (par)
    (div @para) {
        DUNGEON is a game of adventure, danger, and low cunning.  In it
    you will explore some of the most amazing territory ever seen by
    mortal man. Hardened adventurers have run screaming from the terrors
    contained within!
    }
    (par)
    (div @para) {
        In DUNGEON the intrepid explorer delves into the forgotten
    secrets of a lost labyrinth deep in the bowels of the earth,
    searching for vast treasures long hidden from prying eyes, treasures
    guarded by fearsome monsters and diabolical traps!
    }
    (par)
    (div @para) {
        No PDP-10 should be without one!
    }
    (par)
    (div @para) {
        DUNGEON was created at the Programming Technology Division of the
    MIT Laboratory for Computer Science, by Tim Anderson, Marc Blank,
    Bruce Daniels, and Dave Lebling.  It was inspired by the ADVENTURE
    game of Crowther and Woods, and Dungeons and Dragons, by Gygax and
    Arneson.  DUNGEON is written in MDL \(alias MUDDLE\).
    }
    (par)
    (div @para) {
    Direct inquiries by Net mail to DUNGEON\@MIT-DMS.
    }

#front-door
(name *)        front door
(door *)
(* is #in #west-of-house)
(prevent [enter *])
    (narrate door is locked)
(prevent [open *])
    The door cannot be opened.
(prevent [unlock *])
    It doesn't seem to work.
%% Check going east from West of House: say "The door is locked, and there is evidently no key." instead.

#house
(name *)        white house
(#west-of-house attracts *)
(#north-of-house attracts *)
(#south-of-house attracts *)
(#east-of-house attracts *)
(descr *)
    The house is a beautiful colonial house which is painted white.
    It is clear that the owners must have been extremely wealthy.
~(unlikely [enter *])

#north-of-house
(name *)        North of House
(room *)
(* is sacred)
(from * go #west to #west-of-house)
(from * go #east to #east-of-house)
(from * go #north to #forest3)
(narrate failing to leave * #south)
    (narrate windows are barred)
(look *)
    You are facing the north side of a white house.
    There is no door here, and all the windows are barred.
(unlikely [enter *])
(unlikely [go to *])
(very unlikely [go to *])

#south-of-house
(name *)        South of House
(room *)
(* is sacred)
(from * go #west to #west-of-house)
(from * go #east to #east-of-house)
(from * go #south to #forest2)
(narrate failing to leave * #north)
    (narrate windows are barred)
(look *)
    You are facing the south side of a white house.
    There is no door here, and all the windows are barred.
(unlikely [enter *])
(unlikely [go to *])
(very unlikely [go to *])

#east-of-house
(name *)        Behind House
(room *)
(* is sacred)
(from * go #north to #north-of-house)
(from * go #south to #south-of-house)
(from * go #east to #clearing)
(look *)
    You are behind the white house.
    In one corner of the house is a window that is
    (if)(#window is open)(then)
        slightly ajar.
    (else)
        open.
    (endif)
(unlikely [enter *])
(unlikely [go to *])
(very unlikely [go to *])
(instead of [enter #house])
    (current room *)
    (try [go #west])

#window
(name *)        window
(door *)
(openable *)
(from #east-of-house go #west through #window to #kitchen)
(from #kitchen go #east through #window to #east-of-house)
(* blocks passage)
	(* is closed)
(perform [climb *])
    (try [enter *])
(narrate opening *)
    With great effort, you open the window far enough to allow entry.
(narrate closing *)
    The window closes \(more easily than it opened\).

#kitchen
(name *)        Kitchen
(room *)
(* is sacred)
(* is in the house)
(* awards 10 points on first visit)
(from * go #west to #living-room)
(from * go #up to #attic)
(look *)
    This is the kitchen of the white house.
    A table seems to have been used recently for the preparation of food.
    A passage leads to the west and a dark staircase can be seen leading upward.
    A dark chimney leads down and to the east is a small window which is
    (if)(#window is open)(then)
        open.
    (else)
        closed.
    (endif)

(prevent [go #down])
    (current room *)
    Only Santa Claus climbs down chimneys.

#sack
